1
00:00:00,093 --> 00:00:05,285
Meet Zerant, a privacy-preserving trust layer for applications built around Zcash.

2
00:00:05,238 --> 00:00:11,999
Too often, an app asks people to hand over their full identity or wallet activity when it only needs one reliable fact.

3
00:00:11,999 --> 00:00:19,132
Zerant takes a different approach: issue a trusted credential, request a specific claim, and let the holder decide what to share.

4
00:00:19,623 --> 00:00:22,609
The holder signs into Zerant with a passkey.

5
00:00:22,563 --> 00:00:25,104
No wallet connection is required.

6
00:00:25,104 --> 00:00:31,002
Inside the private vault, Privacy Builders Collective has issued a Contributor Eligibility credential.

7
00:00:31,002 --> 00:00:35,521
The holder can see that they are eligible without publishing their credential history.

8
00:00:35,521 --> 00:00:40,772
Their Zerant ID is for trust and account access, not a Zcash payment address.

9
00:00:41,253 --> 00:00:46,528
Now Privacy Builders Rewards Portal requests one narrow fact: Contributor Eligibility.

10
00:00:46,482 --> 00:00:54,070
Before any disclosure, the holder can review the requester, the exact purpose, the credential issuer, and the single claim value.

11
00:00:54,070 --> 00:00:57,390
The review clearly explains what remains private.

12
00:00:57,390 --> 00:01:01,682
The holder may deny the request or explicitly approve this one result.

13
00:01:01,682 --> 00:01:04,211
Approval is never automatic.

14
00:01:04,690 --> 00:01:09,570
The verifier validates the approved proof and receives the bounded answer: Eligible.

15
00:01:09,524 --> 00:01:17,087
It does not receive the holder's complete source credential, unrelated claims, wallet address, balance, or transaction history.

16
00:01:17,087 --> 00:01:26,090
Zerant also checks the trust relationship, expiry, revocation, intended audience, and replay conditions before treating that result as verified.

17
00:01:26,565 --> 00:01:29,191
Payment routing is a separate step.

18
00:01:29,144 --> 00:01:36,337
The holder may share a temporary, shielded-capable Zcash testnet destination with a trusted organization for a stated payout purpose.

19
00:01:36,337 --> 00:01:42,798
The address is encrypted, scoped to that one relationship, and it can be withdrawn or expires automatically.

20
00:01:42,798 --> 00:01:47,233
It never becomes the holder's Zerant identity or a general verifier claim.

21
00:01:47,725 --> 00:01:56,969
An authorized organization operator sees a private payout reference and purpose, not a reusable holder identifier or an exposed receive address in the general inbox.

22
00:01:56,922 --> 00:02:02,880
When the operator chooses an amount, Zerant prepares a payment against that specific active payout record.

23
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:06,692
This does not move funds or give Zerant spending authority.

24
00:02:07,167 --> 00:02:16,027
Zerant validates the network, recipient, and exact amount, then prepares a canonical ZIP three two one payment request with a reviewed link and QR code.

25
00:02:15,980 --> 00:02:21,039
A compatible external Zcash wallet holds the spending keys and authorizes the transaction.

26
00:02:21,039 --> 00:02:28,136
Preparing a request is not submitting a transaction, and a transaction ID alone is not proof that a shielded payment has settled.

27
00:02:28,618 --> 00:02:37,538
The same trust and payment workflows remain accessible on smaller screens, with grouped navigation, a keyboard-accessible menu, and careful loading and error states.

28
00:02:37,491 --> 00:02:41,567
The product keeps the identity and payment boundaries clear throughout.

29
00:02:42,054 --> 00:02:51,621
From private credentials to selective proof, and from temporary payout routing to wallet handoff, Zerant keeps trust, consent, and spending authority separate.

30
00:02:51,575 --> 00:02:53,013
Zerant.

31
00:02:53,013 --> 00:02:54,643
Prove trust.

32
00:02:54,643 --> 00:02:56,465
Preserve privacy.
